  "configuration_id": "config_049",
  "reason": "Evidence: The largest performance jump came from switching RNA encoder from pca_n2000 to scvi_n200 (0.639→0.678 with resnet50; 0.635→0.706 with ViT-L), establishing that learned RNA representations are far superior to PCA. The current best (config_037: scvi_n200, ViT-L, avgpool, weighted, 0.706 acc) has not been challenged by another learned encoder. Question: config_049 swaps only the RNA encoder to scgpt while holding all other settings at their empirically best values (multiprofile, ViT-L, avgpool, weighted). This directly tests whether scgpt—a different learned, generative-style RNA representation—can match or exceed scvi_n200, or whether scvi has already captured the key biological signal. Why this is the best next experiment: It explores the single most impactful untested design axis (RNA encoder identity) under the strongest known configuration, giving a clean A/B comparison against config_037. If scgpt underperforms, we confirm scvi as the optimal encoder and can focus remaining budget on fine-tuning aggregation/fusion. If it matches or exceeds, we open a new promising direction. This is more informative than testing transformer aggregation (where PCA evidence already strongly favors avgpool) or concat fusion (where weighted has consistently won)."
}

